###  http://www.github.com/silvialx/Onto/CompanionsInGuiltArgument
ex:CompanionsInGuiltArgument rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Argument> ;
                             <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/hasConclusion> ex:ErrorTheoreticArgumentsProveTooMuchAndShouldBeRejected ;
                             <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/hasPremise> ex:ErrorTheoreticArgumentsTargetQueernessAndDisagreementInMorality ,
                                                                                  ex:MathematicalLogicalAndEpistemicKnowledgeIsNotUndermined ,
                                                                                  ex:TheSameErrorTheoreticArgumentsGeneralizeToMathLogicAndEpistemicReasons ;
                             <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/negates> ex:ErrorTheory ;
                             <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/supports> ex:Nonnaturalism ;
                             rdfs:label "if error-theoretic arguments (queerness, disagreement) also threaten seemingly unproblematic domains like mathematics, epistemic reasons, or logic, then either those domains are equally undermined or the argument proves too much (Cuneo)." .


###  http://www.github.com/silvialx/Onto/Contractualism
ex:Contractualism rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           ex:ConsistentPosition ,
                           ex:MetaethicalPosition ;
                  ex:actionMotivationSupposition ex:Internalist ;
                  ex:cognitivistSupposition ex:Cognitivist ;
                  ex:epistemicSupposition ex:ReflectiveEquilibriumist ;
                  ex:metaphysicalSupposition ex:Quietist ;
                  ex:representationSupposition ex:Representationalist ;
                  ex:truthAptnessSupposition ex:TruthApt ;
                  rdfs:comment "an act is wrong if its performance would be disallowed by any principle that no one could reasonably reject as a basis for informed, unforced general agreement (Scanlon); related contractarian views ground morality in rational bargaining (Gauthier) or mutual accountability (Darwall)." .


###  http://www.github.com/silvialx/Onto/ConvergenceInMoralityAndScience
ex:ConvergenceInMoralityAndScience rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                            <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Claim> ;
                                   <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/negates> ex:SimilaritiesInMoralityAndScience ;
                                   <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/supports> ex:DifferenceInMoralityAndScience ;
                                   rdfs:label "Convergence is understood differently in science and morality. Firstly, no one expects full convergence in a moral inquiry, whereas it should ideally be reached in an inquiry of scientific nature. Even if it happened that full convergence in ethical outlook was reached, it will not be correct to think it has come about because convergence has been guided by how things really are, while convergence in the sicences might be explained in that way." .


