###  http://www.github.com/silvialx/Onto/HumeLaw
ex:HumeLaw rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                    <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Argument> ;
           dcterms:creator ex:Hume ;
           <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/hasConclusion> ex:OughtCannotBeDerivedFromIsAlone ;
           <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/hasPremise> ex:DescriptivePremisesStateOnlyWhatIsTheCase ,
                                                                ex:NoValidArgumentReachesAPurelyNormativeConclusionFromPurelyDescriptivePremises ,
                                                                ex:NormativeConclusionsStateWhatOughtToBeTheCase ;
           <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/negates> ex:NaturalRealism ,
                                                             ex:NonReductiveNaturalism ;
           <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/supports> ex:Noncognitivist ;
           schema:about ex:HumeLaw ;
           rdfs:label "no purely descriptive set of premises (statements of 'is') can logically entail a normative conclusion (a statement of 'ought') without at least one normative premise smuggled in; this gap tells against naturalist views that try to derive moral facts from natural ones, and is often read as support for noncognitivism (Hume, the is-ought gap)." .


###  http://www.github.com/silvialx/Onto/HumeanConstructivism
ex:HumeanConstructivism rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 ex:ConsistentPosition ,
                                 ex:MetaethicalPosition ;
                        ex:actionMotivationSupposition ex:Internalist ;
                        ex:cognitivistSupposition ex:Cognitivist ;
                        ex:epistemicSupposition ex:ProceduralConstructivist ;
                        ex:metaphysicalSupposition ex:MetaphysicalConstructivist ;
                        ex:representationSupposition ex:Representationalist ;
                        ex:truthAptnessSupposition ex:TruthApt ;
                        rdfs:comment "normative truths are constituted by what follows from an agent's own evaluative attitudes under idealized coherence; motivated in part by an evolutionary debunking argument against realist alternatives (Sharon Street)." .


