                                                    ex:Internalist ;
                   rdfs:comment "Weak internalism holds that moral judgment is only defeasibly or normally connected to motivation; amoralists are conceptually possible but psychologically abnormal." .


###  http://www.github.com/silvialx/Onto/WhyBeMoralArgument
ex:WhyBeMoralArgument rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Argument> ;
                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/hasConclusion> ex:InternalismBetterExplainsThePracticalAuthorityOfMoralRequirements ;
                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/hasPremise> ex:ExternalismTreatsTheConnectionBetweenJudgmentAndMotivationAsMerelyContingent ,
                                                                           ex:IfThatConnectionIsMerelyContingentItIsPuzzlingWhyMoralityShouldBeAuthoritative ,
                                                                           ex:MoralConsiderationsAreTakenToBePracticallyAuthoritative ;
                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/negates> ex:Externalist ,
                                                                        ex:Nonnaturalism ;
                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/supports> ex:Internalist ;
                      rdfs:label "if the connection between recognizing a moral requirement and being moved by it is merely contingent, it becomes puzzling why moral considerations should be practically authoritative at all; this puzzle is often taken to favour internalism (Prichard)." .


