###  http://www.semanticweb.org/silvi/ontologies/2026/6/untitled-ontology-6#ArgumentForQueerness
:ArgumentForQueerness rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Argument> ;
                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/negates> ex:NaturalRealism ,
                                                                        ex:NonnaturalRealism ,
                                                                        ex:Realist ;
                      <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/supports> ex:Fictionalist ;
                      rdfs:label "The argument from queerness claims that objective moral values would be so strange and unlike any other properties in the universe that we have no good reason to believe they exist." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/silvi/ontologies/2026/6/untitled-ontology-6#ArgumentForRelativity
:ArgumentForRelativity rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Argument> ;
                       <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/negates> ex:Realist ;
                       <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/supports> ex:Fictionalist ,
                                                                          ex:Relativist ;
                       rdfs:label "The argument from relativity has as its premiss the well-known variation in moral codes from one society to another and from one period to another, and also the differences in moral beliefs between different groups and classes within a complex community. Such variation is in itself merely a truth of descriptive morality, a fact of anthropology which entails neither first order nor second order ethical views (Mackie 1977)." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/silvi/ontologies/2026/6/untitled-ontology-6#CognitiveCommand
:CognitiveCommand rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Claim> ;
                  rdfs:label "A discourse exhibits Cognitive Command if and only if it is a priori that differences of opinion arising ehithin it can be explained only in terms of \"divergent input\"." .


###  http://www.semanticweb.org/silvi/ontologies/2026/6/untitled-ontology-6#CultureAndEthicalKnowledge
:CultureAndEthicalKnowledge rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                     <http://www.github.com/argumentsontology/Claim> ;
                            rdfs:label "society can posses ethical knowledge only regarding the nest of beliefs implied by the way of living, the culture, of dais society. Comparing the beliefs entailed in a culture with the ones of another does not allow one to acquire ethical knowledge, if for \"ethical knowledge\" we mean the knowledge involved in making judgements, pursuing actions. In other words, reflecting on one's culture by comparing it with another will not give any insights on our own ethical notions." .


